hey're here  permanently with legal status but  granted the people that are not granted  asylum will also in effect be granted  asylum because they won't be removed  under any circumstances whatsoever and  that's one of the tricks with this  administration they talk about they'll  be put in deportation proceedings if  they're not granted to the asylum but  they won't actually be deported the the  line the list of uh people that have not  been deported it's over what i think  it's one and a half million people so  they'll just be put on that pile and and  at the rate of deporting 59 000 a year  you know we're looking at 25 years to  get get to the people that will be uh  order deported eventually jonathan what  are the the differences in  qualifications um for somebody who may  not know  with the asylum officers versus the  asylum judges and how could that be  dangerous or kind of muddy with  information that's given out  well i think some of the things that are  dangerous is if it's asylum officers  that are  you know that are trained that there's  no review of what they do so if they are  granting asylum to people that don't  deserve asylum  there's no way to review that there's no  court that there's no judge that's been  trained on this there's no it doesn't  look like there's even an adversarial  process in there it's just simply if one  of these  agents  of the uscis decides somebody grants it  they're going to they're going to be  granted asylum and it really should be a  very thorough process because asylum is  simply not  coming to this country saying you'd  prefer to live here than where you  currently live because you'll be  more prosperous here  these are people that are supposedly  fleeing persecution where they are and  this administration knows by and large  all these massive uh migration patterns  that have happened over the past year  and a half are not people fleeing  persecution they're people  fleeing to the united states because  they know they have joe biden as the  president and he's going to welcome them  with open arms and give them every right  privilege and benefit that american  citizens receive yeah to that point  asylum is a pretty tough standard to  reach 31 000 people were granted asylum  in fiscal year 2021.  under this new approach where judges are  essentially removed from the equation  jonathan how high will that number go  when you have look at the screen right  now a million people encountered at the  border in fiscal year 2022 so far  yeah you figure it's the design the  purpose of this is to  want to encourage more people to come  because they'll know it's going to be  easier but my guess is it will be lower  in that 90 percent number um you know so  if it does get lower than a 90  number the floodgates are even wider  open and also people will be granted  asylum very quickly and if they're not  growing asylum quickly they'll just be  put in the same old process and they're  no worse off you know in fact they're  they're you know they're they're going  to be staying in this country too so  yeah this will my guess is it'll be far  lower than what the normal standard is  and  the administration now will control what  that number is because they could train  people  to to let in as many people as they want  they can loosen the standards as much as  they want and we know that's what they  want to do this has been a political  move all along and they want as many  illegal aliens to come into the country  as possible and they're going to do  everything they can to do it and you  know maybe some of this is to make it  look like they're doing something to  protect some democrats in the midterms  but you know this is their their plan is  being executed as well as anything  they're doing in this administration  which is open borders and embassy and  unfortunately we're going to see how  that all plays out former ice director  jonathan fahey thanks for getting up  with us early on this memorial day 